    If kind is 0 then,
        Return a text, 'literal integer'.
    If kind is 1 then,
        Return a text, 'literal float'.
    If kind is 2 then,
        Return a text, 'literal text'.
    Return a text, 'literal boolean'.

(Unconditional concatenation only - see the header comment above for
 why no caller ever wraps the `For each` in an `If`.)

To 'gen concat two blocks' with a list called 'the first block' and a list called 'the second block'.
    a list called 'the combined' is [].
    For each line in 'the first block', append line to 'the combined'.
    For each line in 'the second block', append line to 'the combined'.
    Return a list, 'the combined'.

To 'gen concat three blocks' with a list called 'the first block' and a list called 'the second block' and a list called 'the third block'.
    a list called 'the combined' is [].
    For each line in 'the first block', append line to 'the combined'.
    For each line in 'the second block', append line to 'the combined'.
    For each line in 'the third block', append line to 'the combined'.
    Return a list, 'the combined'.

(FIL-10, FIL-11, FIL-12, FIL-13, FIL-14: length, size (a documented
 synonym for length), empty, first and last.

 Two lists are declared every time - one forced to `[]` and one forced
 to at least one element - because FIL-12's claim needs both a true and
 a false `empty` reading to mean anything, and which block is WRITTEN
 FIRST is drawn (the row's own "random which is emitted first"). The
 populated list's element type (number, float, text or boolean) and its
 count (1..5, so the one-element case where first and last coincide is
 reachable) are both drawn; each element is 'gen typed literal's own
 unbounded random spelling, reused VERBATIM as the comparison operand
 for FIL-13/FIL-14, so the assertion checks the generator's writing
 against itself rather than a second, independently-fallible reading of
 the manual.

 first/last on the EMPTY list are read too, but NOT asserted:
 LANGUAGE.md's List Properties table promises a value for a populated
 list, and says nothing about what first/last report on one with no
 items - unlike element-by-index access, which the Bounds Checking
 paragraph explicitly covers (LANGUAGE.md:3883-3886, just past the
 code fence). Hand-verified
 against 0.4.14: both set the error flag and read back 0, the same
 default-value shape the documented bounds-checking behaviour uses for
 an out-of-range index - but nothing in the manual says so for these
 two properties specifically, so the read here is exercised for
 crash-safety only. Recorded in the worker report, not as a ledger row.)

