(--- The ledger's file leaves, batch B, 2026-08-29 ----------------------

 docs/ledger/files.md maps LANGUAGE.md's File I/O chapter, lines
 3762-4158, into numbered claims;
 everything below puts one of those claims on trial and ASSERTS its
 documented result, exit 95 and `ASSERT <ID>: expected <x> got <y>` on
 failure, the same as every other ledger leaf. Reuses gen_buffers.vox's
 name allocator ('gen buffer name' / 'gen buffer reference' / 'gen
 buffer property' / 'gen buffer companion') and assertion helpers ('gen
 assert number is' / 'gen assert number matches' / 'gen assert true' /
 'gen assert false') rather than copying them - both are surface-neutral
 despite their names (PROCEDURE.md 6a). Every open below targets a path
 built from gen_scratch_flag, the per-run scratch directory the harness
 hands this program on the command line - the same D16 guard 'gen leaf
 file round trip' uses, repeated in each leaf below rather than shared,
 since a leaf that forgets its own guard is exactly the failure mode the
 guard exists to catch. Never a path the generator assembled itself,
 never anything outside that directory.

 A property read AFTER Close does not crash, but it does not report the
 true value either - it reads -1 (found by hand while building FIL-40's
 probe: checking size straight after Close read -1, not the 5 bytes
 actually written). Every assertion below therefore runs on the
 still-open handle, immediately after the operation it is about, before
 any Close.

 Rows covered here: FIL-01, FIL-02, FIL-03, FIL-04, FIL-05, FIL-06,
 FIL-07, FIL-40, FIL-46. Retained probes:
 docs/ledger/probes/files/FIL-02.vox, FIL-04.vox, FIL-06.vox, FIL-07.vox,
 FIL-40.vox, FIL-53.vox (the last also covers FIL-46) - each is the
 exact rendered assertion shape, run clean and then deliberately broken
 to confirm the Exit 95 path.)

(A range assertion: 'gen assert number is'/'matches' in gen_buffers.vox
 only ever test equality, and two of this batch's claims - FIL-02's
 descriptor, FIL-05/FIL-06's timestamps - are knowable only as a floor
 or a ceiling, never as a literal number the generator could have
 written itself. 'the minimum'/'the maximum' are EXPRESSIONS, not always
 literals - 'current time's unix' for the not-in-the-future half,
 hand-verified against 0.4.14 to work inline inside an If condition the
 same way the manual's own inline Print example uses it
 (LANGUAGE.md:4590, the "Inline Time Access" example; docs/ledger/
 probes/files/FIL-06.vox.).)

To 'gen file assert at least' with a text called 'the row' and a text called 'the subject' and a text called 'the minimum' and a text called 'the minimum shown'.
    a text called 'the got shown' is 'gen buffer slot of' of 'the subject'.
    a text called 'the line' is "If {'the subject'} is less than {'the minimum'} then, Print \"ASSERT {'the row'}: expected at least {'the minimum shown'} got {'the got shown'}\", Exit 95. Print {'the subject'}".
    Return a text, 'the line'.

(A two-sided range assertion whose SUCCESS path prints a fixed marker
 rather than the subject - unlike 'gen file assert at least' above,
 which prints the observed value because a descriptor number is
 deterministic across the oracle's two runs of one binary. A file's
 modified/accessed timestamp is not: it can tick a wall-clock second
 between those two runs (a fresh write on each run, no guarantee both
 land in the same second), and a first version of this leaf that
 printed the raw value was caught by a live campaign (200 programs,
 seed 5001) as a `nondeterministic` finding within 20 programs - a
 defect in the leaf, not in the compiler. The bound-vs-subject checks
 are exactly as strict; only what prints on success differs, the same
 tradeoff 'gen leaf timer and clock' already makes (src/gen_misc.vox)
 for the identical reason.)

To 'gen file assert range quietly' with a text called 'the row' and a text called 'the subject' and a text called 'the minimum' and a text called 'the minimum shown' and a text called 'the maximum' and a text called 'the maximum shown'.
    a text called 'the got shown' is 'gen buffer slot of' of 'the subject'.
    a text called 'the low line' is "If {'the subject'} is less than {'the minimum'} then, Print \"ASSERT {'the row'}: expected at least {'the minimum shown'} got {'the got shown'}\", Exit 95.".
    a text called 'the high line' is "If {'the subject'} is greater than {'the maximum'} then, Print \"ASSERT {'the row'}: expected at most {'the maximum shown'} got {'the got shown'}\", Exit 95.".
    a text called 'the line' is "{'the low line'} {'the high line'} Print \"{'the row'} in range\"".
    Return a text, 'the line'.

